+# Self-process proc broker contract
+
+Authority: user-approved bounded Chromium namespace broker expansion, 2026-10-30.
+
+The per-command supervisor must preserve Landlock and native Chromium sandboxing.
+It may inject read-only descriptors for the requesting process's `stat` and `maps`.
+It must resolve identity per notification, including inherited child filters.
+
+User approval on 2026-10-30 additionally permits a read-only proc-root directory
+anchor and read-only enumeration of the requesting process's fd directory.
+The latter may be absolute or relative self/fd/ against a verified proc-root FD.
+Reopening the same verified proc-root anchor with openat dot is equivalent.
+This exposes root directory entries/PIDs and self FD numbers, but grants no proc
+subtree rule, FD-content reopening, sensitive proc file access or write authority.
+
+Namespace setup may affect only a new descendant user namespace owned by the
+supervisor's unprivileged UID, within its namespace ancestry, and the requesting
+process. The original namespace and namespaces owned by others are excluded.
+Only `deny` for setgroups and a single identity-preserving UID/GID mapping are
+permitted. Mapping descriptors exposed to the command must be sealed placeholders,
+never writable proc descriptors. The supervisor copies and validates bounded write
+contents, pins process/namespace identity, validates the live notification, and
+performs the fixed operation. Kernel write-once and namespace mapping checks remain.
+Unsupported operations, identities, ABI, flags and mapping contents fail closed.
+For nested namespaces Linux requires mapping writes from the immediate parent
+user namespace. A short-lived fixed-operation helper may enter only the pinned,
+verified owned parent namespace, preserve its original numeric UID/GID, perform
+the already validated write, and exit. The supervisor must remain in its original
+namespace. Helper operations must be bounded, reaped, and fail closed.
+No blanket proc rule, namespace policy relaxation, or browser-cache write is allowed.
+
+Qualification requires real-kernel denial and race regressions plus actual MCP
+Playwright navigation, JavaScript, screenshot and process/profile cleanup. Direct
+and child native Node memoryUsage must remain working.
